# Converted to the revised ten-section template (plus section 11); content and tables carried from the previous version.
BOOK = 'Colossians'
TESTAMENT = 'New Testament'
GENRE = 'letters'
SUBTITLE = 'The Preeminent Christ, the Mystery, and Completeness in Him'
SECTIONS = [(1, 'Book Setting & Program Identity', [('p', 'This section introduces you to the book by explaining who wrote it, who received it, and what was happening when it was written. It also shows you which of God’s programs the book belongs to — Israel’s prophetic program or the Body of Christ’s mystery program. Knowing the setting and the program identity helps you read the book in its proper context and understand how its teachings fit into the larger story of Scripture.'), ('p', 'Colossians is a letter from “Paul, an apostle of Jesus Christ by the will of God, and Timotheus our brother, to the saints and faithful brethren in Christ which are at Colosse” (Col. 1:1–2). Paul writes from bonds (Col. 4:3, 18) to warn the believers against philosophy, tradition, and ordinances that would take them from Christ, and to show them what they have in Him (Col. 2:8, 16–17).'), ('box', 'Program Identity:', 'Colossians belongs to the Mystery Program: it speaks of the mystery hid from ages and generations and now made manifest to the saints, and addresses believers in Christ at Colosse (Col. 1:26–27; 1:2).')]),
(2, 'Narrative Flow of Colossians', [('p', 'This section gives you a simple walkthrough of how the book unfolds. Instead of diving into details right away, it shows the major movements in the letter — how Paul begins, how his instructions develop, and how the letter builds toward its close. Seeing the flow of the book helps you understand the big picture before you study the individual passages.'), ('h3', 'Movement 1 — Thanksgiving and Prayer (Ch. 1:1–14)'), ('p', 'Paul thanks God for the believers’ faith and love, and prays that they be filled with the knowledge of His will (Col. 1:3–14).'), ('h3', 'Movement 2 — The Preeminence of Christ and the Mystery (1:15–2:7)'), ('p', 'Christ is the image of the invisible God and head of the body, and the mystery is “Christ in you, the hope of glory” (Col. 1:15–20, 26–27).'), ('h3', 'Movement 3 — Complete in Christ (2:8–23)'), ('p', 'Believers are complete in Him, and Paul warns against philosophy, ordinances, and the shadow of things to come (Col. 2:9–10, 16–17).'), ('h3', 'Movement 4 — The Walk Above (3:1–4:6)'), ('p', 'Paul calls believers to set their affections above, to put off the old man and put on the new, and to order home and speech (Col. 3:1–4, 9–10, 18–4:1; 4:2–6).'), ('h3', 'Movement 5 — Greetings and Closing (4:7–18)'), ('p', 'Paul names his fellow workers, sends greetings, and closes with “Remember my bonds. Grace be with you” (Col. 4:7–18).')]),
(3, 'Program Themes in Colossians', [('p', 'This section highlights the major ideas Paul emphasizes in this book — the big doctrinal themes that shape how the Body of Christ should think and live. These themes are not verse-by-verse explanations; they are the book’s main emphases, the repeated ideas Paul wants believers to carry forward. Seeing these themes together helps you understand the book’s message before you look at the individual truths in the tables.'), ('ol', ['<b>The Mystery Made Manifest</b> — The mystery which hath been hid from ages and from generations is made manifest to His saints (Col. 1:26).', '<b>Christ in You</b> — The riches of the glory of this mystery among the Gentiles is “Christ in you, the hope of glory” (Col. 1:27).', '<b>Complete in Christ</b> — In Christ dwells all the fulness of the Godhead bodily, and believers are complete in Him (Col. 2:9–10).', '<b>Risen with Christ</b> — Believers are risen with Christ, their life is hid with Christ in God, and they are to seek things above (Col. 3:1–3).', '<b>Appearing in Glory</b> — When Christ, who is our life, shall appear, they also shall appear with Him in glory (Col. 3:4).'])]),
(4, 'Christ in Colossians', [('p', 'This section gathers what the letter teaches about Christ — who He is, what He has done, and how Paul presents Him to the church. These statements help you see the book’s Christ-centered focus before you study the doctrinal truths in detail. By collecting these verses together, you get a clear picture of how Colossians lifts up Christ.'), ('ol', ['<b>Image of the Invisible God</b> — Christ is the image of the invisible God, the firstborn of every creature (Col. 1:15).', '<b>Creator and Sustainer</b> — By Him were all things created, and by Him all things consist (Col. 1:16–17).', '<b>Head of the Body</b> — He is the head of the body, the church, the beginning, the firstborn from the dead (Col. 1:18).', '<b>Reconciler by the Blood of His Cross</b> — God reconciled all things to Himself by Him, having made peace through the blood of His cross (Col. 1:20–22).', '<b>Head of All Principality and Power</b> — Christ is the head of all principality and power, and He triumphed over them in His cross (Col. 2:10, 15).'])]),
(5, 'The Church, the Law, and Israel in Colossians', [('p', 'This section helps you see how Colossians speaks about the church, the law, and Israel without mixing their programs. By gathering these points together, this section shows you how to read Colossians in its own setting — as a Body-of-Christ letter — while recognizing what it says (and does not say) about Israel’s program.'), ('p', 'Colossians speaks to believers about circumcision and Jewish observances without calling the readers Israel:'), ('ul', ['Believers are circumcised with “the circumcision made without hands” in the putting off of the body of the sins of the flesh (Col. 2:11).', 'Paul warns against being judged in meat, drink, a holyday, the new moon, or the sabbath days, “which are a shadow of things to come; but the body is of Christ” (Col. 2:16–17).', '“There is neither Greek nor Jew, circumcision nor uncircumcision” in the new man where Christ is all and in all (Col. 3:11).', 'The letter does not list Israel’s covenants or kingdom promises as the readers’ own.']), ('p', 'This reading rests on the passage’s own audience markers and setting.')]),
(6, 'Body-of-Christ Distinctions', [('p', 'This section highlights the teachings in Colossians that belong uniquely to the Body of Christ — truths Paul gives to the church in this present age. By gathering these together, this section helps you see how Colossians contributes to the doctrine and order of the Body of Christ without borrowing from Israel’s prophetic program.'), ('p', 'Colossians teaches the Body’s standing in Christ:'), ('ul', ['Christ as head of the body, the church', 'the mystery of Christ in you, the hope of glory', 'completeness in Christ and risen life with Him', 'the new man in whom there is neither Greek nor Jew', 'a walk above, in the things of Christ']), ('p', 'It does not give Israel’s covenants, earthly kingdom, or Temple worship to the Body.')]),
(7, 'Truths Table: What Colossians Says', [('p', 'This table gathers the key statements, instructions, and teachings in Colossians and shows which ones belong to the Body of Christ. Each row lists a truth from the letter, explains what it says, and identifies its program identity based on Paul’s audience and setting. This helps you see, at a glance, what Colossians teaches directly to the church today and keeps those truths separate from universal principles or reports that apply more broadly.'), ('table', ['Category', 'Truth Revealed in Colossians', 'Program Identity', 'Scripture'], [['The Mystery', 'The mystery hid from ages is now made manifest to His saints.', 'Body of Christ (Pauline) Truth', 'Col. 1:26'], ['Christ in You', 'Christ in you, the hope of glory.', 'Body of Christ (Pauline) Truth', 'Col. 1:27'], ['Head of the Body', 'Christ is head of the body, the church.', 'Body of Christ (Pauline) Truth', 'Col. 1:18'], ['Completeness', 'Believers are complete in Christ.', 'Body of Christ (Pauline) Truth', 'Col. 2:10'], ['Circumcision Without Hands', 'The putting off of the body of the sins of the flesh.', 'Body of Christ (Pauline) Truth', 'Col. 2:11'], ['Risen with Christ', 'Believers are risen with Christ and their life is hid with Him in God.', 'Body of Christ (Pauline) Truth', 'Col. 3:1–3'], ['Appearing in Glory', 'Believers shall appear with Christ in glory.', 'Body of Christ (Pauline) Truth', 'Col. 3:4'], ['The New Man', 'Neither Greek nor Jew, circumcision nor uncircumcision.', 'Body of Christ (Pauline) Truth', 'Col. 3:10–11'], ['Paul’s Stewardship', 'Paul was made a minister according to the dispensation given to him.', 'Body of Christ (Pauline) Truth', 'Col. 1:25']], [22, 43, 17, 18])]),
(8, 'Universal Truths Table (All People, All Time)', [('p', 'This table gathers the truths in Colossians that apply to everyone, not just the Body of Christ. These are principles rooted in God’s character — things that hold true across all programs and all ages. By separating these universal truths from Paul’s program-specific instructions, this section helps you see what Colossians teaches all believers everywhere and what it teaches the church in its own setting.'), ('table', ['Universal Truth', 'Description', 'Scripture'], [['Christ the Creator', 'All things were created by Him and for Him.', 'Col. 1:16'], ['God’s Forgiveness', 'Forgiving one another, even as Christ forgave you.', 'Col. 3:13'], ['Love as the Bond', 'Above all, put on charity, the bond of perfectness.', 'Col. 3:14'], ['Thankfulness', 'Be ye thankful.', 'Col. 3:15'], ['The Word Dwelling Richly', 'Let the word of Christ dwell in you richly.', 'Col. 3:16'], ['Working Heartily', 'Whatsoever ye do, do it heartily, as to the Lord.', 'Col. 3:23'], ['Fair Treatment', 'Masters, give that which is just and equal.', 'Col. 4:1'], ['Gracious Speech', 'Let your speech be alway with grace, seasoned with salt.', 'Col. 4:6']], [30, 46, 24])]),
(9, 'Program-Safe Application Grid', [('p', 'This section shows how to apply the teachings of Colossians without mixing programs. The applications come directly from Paul’s instructions to the Body of Christ and from the universal truths that apply to everyone. By keeping the applications tied to what the letter actually says — and not borrowing commands from Israel’s covenants or prophetic program — this grid helps you respond to Colossians in a way that is faithful to its setting, clear, and safe.'), ('ol', ['<b>Keep Christ first</b> — Let Him have the preeminence in all things (Col. 1:18).', '<b>Know its completeness</b> — Do not return to philosophy or ordinances (Col. 2:8–10).', '<b>Seek things above</b> — Set the affections on things above (Col. 3:1–2).', '<b>Put off and put on</b> — Lay aside the old man and put on the new (Col. 3:9–10).', '<b>Forgive and love</b> — Forgive as Christ forgave; put on charity (Col. 3:13–14).', '<b>Be thankful</b> — Let the word of Christ dwell richly (Col. 3:15–16).', '<b>Work heartily</b> — Do it as to the Lord (Col. 3:23).', '<b>Speak with grace</b> — Season speech with salt (Col. 4:6).'])]),
(10, 'Teaching Outline for Colossians', [('p', 'This outline gives you a teacher-ready structure for presenting the book. It follows the narrative movements and highlights the major themes, making it easy to teach Colossians in classes, small groups, or personal study.'), ('olr', ['Thanksgiving and Prayer (1:1–14) — Faith, love, and knowledge of God’s will. <i>(Col. 1:3–14)</i>', 'The Preeminent Christ (1:15–23) — Image, Creator, head of the body, and reconciler. <i>(Col. 1:15–22)</i>', 'The Mystery and Paul’s Stewardship (1:24–2:7) — Christ in you, the hope of glory. <i>(Col. 1:25–27)</i>', 'Complete in Him (2:8–23) — Warnings against philosophy, ordinances, and shadows. <i>(Col. 2:8–10, 16–17)</i>', 'Risen with Christ (3:1–4) — Seek things above. <i>(Col. 3:1–4)</i>', 'The New Man and the Walk (3:5–4:1) — Putting off, putting on, and the household. <i>(Col. 3:9–17; 3:18–4:1)</i>', 'Prayer, Wisdom, and Greetings (4:2–18) — Speech, fellow workers, and closing words. <i>(Col. 4:2–6, 18)</i>'])]),
(11, 'Companion Usage and Study Suggestions', [('h3', 'How to Use This Companion in Personal Study'), ('p', 'This Companion is designed to walk alongside you as you read Colossians, helping you see both the letter’s own setting and the timeless truths rooted in God’s character. Use it slowly, chapter by chapter, letting the narrative movements, themes, and truths deepen your understanding. The Program-Safe Applications will guide your heart toward completeness in Christ, thankfulness, and a walk above, drawn from what Colossians itself says to believers.'), ('h3', 'How to Use This Companion in Small Groups or Classes'), ('p', 'This Companion provides a clear, structured framework that makes teaching Colossians simple and accessible. Each section gives you ready-to-use material: narrative movements for overview, themes for doctrinal clarity, universal truths for discussion, and a pastoral teaching outline for weekly lessons. Use the tables to help your group see what the letter says to its readers and what holds for all people, and use the Program-Safe Application Grid to foster meaningful, Christ-centered conversation.'), ('h3', 'How This Companion Fits Within the Acts Overlap Theology Series'), ('p', 'This Companion is part of a larger series designed to help believers read every book of Scripture without blurring God’s prophetic program for Israel and His mystery program for the Body of Christ. Within the Acts Overlap framework, Colossians stands in the mystery revealed through Paul and speaks directly to the Body of Christ. This Companion shows you how to honor the book’s own setting while drawing out the principles that apply to all believers in every age.')])]
